    \u2014 \u0445\u0440\u0430\u043c\u043e\u0432\u044b\u043c\u0438 \u0447\u0443\u0434\u0435\u0441\u0430\u043c\u0438
    \u0434\u043b\u044f \u0443\u043a\u0440\u0435\u043f\u043b\u0435\u043d\u0438\u044f \u0432\u0435\u0440\u044b.
    \u0418\u0441\u0442\u0438\u043d\u043d\u043e\u0435 \u043f\u0440\u0438\u0437\u043d\u0430\u043d\u0438\u0435
    \u0413\u0435\u0440\u043e\u043d \u043f\u043e\u043b\u0443\u0447\u0438\u043b \u043b\u0438\u0448\u044c
    \u0441\u043f\u0443\u0441\u0442\u044f \u043f\u043e\u0447\u0442\u0438 \u0434\u0432\u0435 \u0442\u044b\u0441\u044f\u0447\u0438
    \u043b\u0435\u0442, \u0432 \u044d\u043f\u043e\u0445\u0443 \u0412\u043e\u0437\u0440\u043e\u0436\u0434\u0435\u043d\u0438\u044f
    \u0438 \u043f\u0440\u043e\u043c\u044b\u0448\u043b\u0435\u043d\u043d\u043e\u0439 \u0440\u0435\u0432\u043e\u043b\u044e\u0446\u0438\u0438.
    \u0412 \u0437\u043d\u0430\u043a \u043f\u0440\u0438\u0437\u043d\u0430\u043d\u0438\u044f \u0435\u0433\u043e
    \u0432\u043a\u043b\u0430\u0434\u0430 \u0432 1976 \u0433\u043e\u0434\u0443 \u0435\u0433\u043e
    \u0438\u043c\u0435\u043d\u0435\u043c \u0431\u044b\u043b \u043d\u0430\u0437\u0432\u0430\u043d
    \u043a\u0440\u0430\u0442\u0435\u0440 \u043d\u0430 \u043e\u0431\u0440\u0430\u0442\u043d\u043e\u0439
    \u0441\u0442\u043e\u0440\u043e\u043d\u0435 \u041b\u0443\u043d\u044b."
  interestingFactsEn: "Heron's biography of Alexandria is hidden, but his inventions and his scientific ideas are a real treasure of remarkable facts, he was a man far ahead of his time, and many of his creations seem almost fantastic to the ancient world.\n\nThe steam turbine, 2,000 years ago, is probably the most famous fact: Geron invented an eolipyl, a device that is considered the first steam turbine in history, and it was a copper ball fixed on the axis above the water bunk, and when the water was heated, the steam went up into the balloon and burst out of the curved nozzles at an enormous rate, forcing the ball to rotate.The reconstruction showed that it could reach 3,500 revolutions per minute, but it was not used as an engine — more like an amazing sight or a temple savage.\n\nThe first vending machine in the world imagine that the first sales machine was invented... to sell holy water in Egyptian temples. The principle was brilliantly simple: a man threw a coin into a special hole. It fell on a pencil platform, the weight of the coin opened a valve, and there was a pump of \"holy\" water to wash. When the coin slipped from the platform, the valve closed.It was a real business approach to making temples more profitable!\n\nThe automatic doors of the Heron temple did not stop on the water machine. He created an automatic door opening system for temples. The secret was in the fire on the altar. When the priest fired fire, the air was heated in a sealed vessel under the altar. By expanding the air, the air shifted the water into a system of balance and balance that opened the massive doors in a smooth manner. When the fire was being put out, the air cooled, and the doors closed.It was the first known mechanism to use thermal energy for automation, working on the principle of feedback, as a modern cybernetics.\n\nThe programd puppet theatre of Heron created sophisticated automatic theatres, which could last up to 10 minutes, and the dolls were \"programmed\" with ropes wrapped up in a spinning shaft with pins. The val rotated around the ropes in the proper sequence, forcing the doll to move, and the decorations to change.The sound of thunder for the play was created by the fall of metal balloons on a hidden drum, and it was essentially a representation of a programmable device.\n\nThe navigator and the odometer for the Roman Empire of Heron was also a brilliant geodesist engineer, who described the dioptr as a precise instrument for measuring angles, the shape of modern theodolite, and it was able to perform complex tasks on the ground: measuring distances to inaccessible objects, laying straight lines, and, above all, building tunnels through mountains, accurately synthesizing work on both sides.The other invention was an odometer, a device to measure the path travelled. It was a cart with wheels of the chosen diameter, whose rotation through a system of tooth wheels triggered the mechanism that dropped the stones into the trays. By the number of stones that fell, it was possible to know exactly the distance travelled, as in the modern taxi.\n\n===Scientific input===In addition to \"toys\", Geron left a significant mark in science: the Heron Formula: School formula for finding a three-way triangle, although he himself referred to Archimedes as her author.Farm principle: In his work on optics, he proved that light spreads along the shortest path, forming a private case of the Farm principle 16 centuries before the Farm itself! \"The Golden Rule of Mechanics\": he described the five simplest mechanisms and established the rule that winning power always loses at a distance.\n\nThe paradox of his legacy is the most amazing and perhaps sad fact: almost all these brilliant inventions were forgotten or rejected in antiquity and in the Middle Ages. They were not applied in a world where all the hard work was done by slaves. Eolipil was just a fun toy, and the machines were temple wonders for building faith.In recognition of his contribution in 1976, the crater on the back of the moon was named by his name."
